 "description": "RUNG 7 \u2014 RETINAL LATERAL INHIBITION, so CENTRE-SURROUND EMERGES. A horizontal cell pools the RetinaCell's radius-1/radius-2 rings over the `neighbour` edges rung 1 declared and never used; the bipolar transmits the RESIDUAL centre - g1*h1 - g2*h2, split into two non-negative rectified halves. The surround gain is LEARNED by anti-Hebbian decorrelation (fixed point = the normal equations = Srinivasan's optimal linear predictor), never hand-set -- so centre-surround FALLS OUT of predictive coding instead of being bolted on as a numpy DoG, and Atick & Redlich's SNR-dependent surround becomes a falsifiable prediction. Ships INERT (lateral=0).",
 "microtheory": {
  "name": "retinal_lateral_inhibition",
  "description": "Predict the centre from the surround; transmit the residual. Centre-surround is redundancy reduction (Atick & Redlich 1992; Srinivasan, Laughlin & Dubs 1982; Barlow), i.e. the substrate's own prediction_minus_observation shape in the first synapse. Rung 6 measured that the complementary split x_OFF = 1 - x_ON is degenerate (C_ON,OFF(d) = -C_ON,ON(d) identically), so the channels carry no independent correlation structure for waves to sculpt (Miller 1994 needs DIFFERENT spatial dependence). Lateral inhibition gives each channel its own spatial dependence -- grown from the grid's adjacency, not handed over as a kernel."
 },
